# Prefer pkexec for sudo auth

On this machine, never use interactive `sudo` (or `sudo -S`) to request privilege escalation. Always use `pkexec` so authentication goes through the PolicyKit GUI prompt.

## Rules

- Use `pkexec` whenever a command needs root or elevated privileges
- Do not run `sudo`, `sudo -i`, `sudo -s`, or `sudo -S`
- Prefer `pkexec <command> [args...]` over wrapping shells unless a root shell is truly required
- If a script or tool hard-codes `sudo`, invoke the elevated parts with `pkexec` instead

## Examples

```bash
# ❌ BAD — terminal sudo password prompt
sudo apt update
sudo systemctl restart nginx
echo "$PASS" | sudo -S whoami

# ✅ GOOD — PolicyKit GUI auth via pkexec
pkexec apt update
pkexec systemctl restart nginx
pkexec whoami
```

## Notes

- `pkexec` may reset environment; pass needed env explicitly when required
- If `pkexec` fails or is unavailable, stop and report the error — do not fall back to `sudo`
